### Describe the feature you'd like to request

The middlewares can currently extend/overwrite the context. They can technically modify the input with `return next({ input: newInput })` in the same manner, but that's not reflected in types in the downstream procedures.

I would like the input type change to be reflected in types, the same way as it happens with the context.

Consider the example:

```ts
const orgProcedure = procedure
  .input(v.object({
    orgId: v.pipe(v.string(), v.uuid()),
    orgAccessKey: v.string(), // Imaginary data
  }))
  .use(async ({ input: { orgId, orgAccessKey, ...input }, next }) => {
    const org = await db.org.findOptional(orgId).select("id", "name")
      .where({ orgAccessKey }) // Imaginary access check
    assertHttp404NotFound(org, "Org not found")
    return next({
      ctx: { org }, // Add org object to context
      input, // Remove orgId and orgAccessKey from input
    })
  })

const editAccount = orgProcedure
  .input(v.object({
    accountId: v.pipe(v.string(), v.uuid()),
    name: v.string(),
    balance: v.number(),
  }))
  .mutation(async ({ ctx: { org }, input: { accountId, ...input } }) => {
    // Here input is { name, balance } in runtime,
    // but it's typed as { orgId, orgAccessKey, name, balance }.
    //
    // The query builder errors because of the extra fields in the type.
    await db.account.find(accountId).where({ orgId: org.id }).update(input)
  })
```

Currently, doing the above is not possible (it works in runtime, but it leads to type errors). As such, currently user can not really alter the input alongside the middleware chain; they must always deal with all input fields.

### Describe the solution you'd like to see

In the example above, I would like the `editAccount` input to properly infer its new shape as coming from the upstream middleware.

### Describe alternate solutions

Currently, all procedures must deal with the entire input object. That means two alternatives:

### 1. Extract parent input as unused vars

```ts
const editAccount = orgProcedure
  .input(v.object({
    accountId: v.pipe(v.string(), v.uuid()),
    name: v.string(),
    balance: v.number(),
  }))
  .mutation(async ({ ctx: { org }, input: { orgId, orgAccessKey, accountId, ...input } }) => {
    await db.account.find(accountId).where({ orgId: org.id }).update(input)
  })
```

Problems:
- Unused vars are not cool.
- If I add a new field to the upstream middleware, I will have to update ALL derived procedures that use the whole input.

### 2. Explicitly extract all needed fields

```ts
const editAccount = orgProcedure
  .input(v.object({
    accountId: v.pipe(v.string(), v.uuid()),
    name: v.string(),
    balance: v.number(),
  }))
  .mutation(async ({ ctx: { org }, input: { accountId, name, balance } }) => {
    await db.account.find(accountId).where({ orgId: org.id }).update({ name, balance })
  })
```

This is more reliable, but then each field must be repeated thrice:
1. in the validator
2. in input extractor
3. in update call

So for 20 fields, that gives 40 extra words. 

### Additional information

_No response_

### 👨‍👧‍👦 Contributing

- [x] 🙋‍♂️ Yes, I'd be down to file a PR implementing this feature!

### Describe the feature you'd like to request

`getParseFn` binds `parseAsync` as soon as a validator exposes it, before it considers `parse`:

```ts
// packages/server/src/unstable-core-do-not-import/parser.ts
if (typeof parser.parseAsync === 'function') {
  // ParserZodEsque
  return parser.parseAsync.bind(parser);
}
```

So every zod schema given to `.input()` or `.output()` validates on zod's async path, even in an app with no async refinement or transform anywhere. On zod 4's interpreter that path costs about twice the sync one. It also cannot reach the compiled parser added in zod 4.5 (`zod/compile`), because the compiled `run` hands any call with an async context back to the interpreter:

```js
// zod/v4/core/compile.js
if (ctx?.async || ctx?.direction === "backward" || ctx?.skipChecks || ctx?.[FALLBACK_FLAG]) {
  return originalRun(payload, ctx);
}
```

Measured on tRPC 11.18.0, zod 4.5.4 and Node 24.21.0, with a page of nine-key objects, in µs per call, median of seven rounds. `parseAsync` is what `getParseFn` binds today. `~standard` is tRPC's own Standard Schema branch, copied as is with its async wrapper. `parse` is a plain synchronous call.

Interpreter:

| items | `parseAsync` | `~standard` | `parse` |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 6.6 | 3.1 | 3.3 |
| 10 | 42.8 | 20.1 | 23.3 |
| 100 | 338 | 162 | 160 |
| 1000 | 3 545 | 1 793 | 1 662 |

With `import 'zod/compile'`:

| items | `parseAsync` | `~standard` | `parse` |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 6.3 | 0.88 | 0.79 |
| 10 | 49.7 | 5.3 | 5.0 |
| 100 | 401 | 41 | 43 |
| 1000 | 3 932 | 335 | 361 |

Today a procedure pays 1.8 to 2.1 times the sync cost on the interpreter, and 8 to 11 times once compilation is on. Turning compilation on even makes procedures slower, by 11 to 19 % from ten items up, because the compiled wrapper sits on every schema node and always defers. The gap between zod's two paths is known upstream and was closed as not planned (colinhacks/zod#3446).

We hit this on a NestJS app with 167 procedures and no async refinement anywhere.

### Describe the solution you'd like to see

Try `parse` first in the `ParserZodEsque` branch, and switch to `parseAsync` for good when a schema refuses to run synchronously:

```ts
if (typeof parser.parseAsync === 'function') {
  // ParserZodEsque
  if (typeof parser.parse !== 'function') {
    return parser.parseAsync.bind(parser);
  }
  let isAsync = false;
  return (value) => {
    if (!isAsync) {
      try {
        return parser.parse(value);
      } catch (cause) {
        // A validation failure carries `issues`. Anything else means the
        // schema needs the async path (async refinement or transform).
        if (Array.isArray((cause as { issues?: unknown } | null)?.issues)) {
          throw cause;
        }
        isAsync = true;
      }
    }
    return parser.parseAsync(value);
  };
}
```

What it keeps:

- `error.cause` is still a `ZodError`, so the formatter in the error formatting docs (`error.cause instanceof ZodError`) keeps working.
- Async refinements and transforms, which are why `parseAsync` was adopted in #1131 and #1173, go through the fallback. I checked an async `.refine()` on zod 4 through `createCaller`: valid input passes, invalid input is rejected with `BAD_REQUEST` and a `ZodError` cause.
- `ParseFn` already allows a synchronous return, and both validation middlewares `await` it.
- `getParseFn` runs once per `.input()` or `.output()` call, so the flag lives with the procedure and costs nothing per request.

The cost lands on schemas that do need async. Their synchronous callbacks run twice on the first call of each procedure (once in the aborted sync attempt, once in `parseAsync`), and only `parseAsync` runs after that. A refinement that throws a non-validation error also moves its procedure to the async path, and the caller still gets the error from that run.

zod uses the same approach in its own Standard Schema `validate`: `safeParse`, then `safeParseAsync` in the `catch` (`zod/v4/core/schemas.js`).

### Describe alternate solutions

1. Check `~standard` before `parseAsync`. Type inference already prefers Standard Schema since #6888, zod 4's `~standard.validate` is sync first, and the tables show this branch running as fast as `parse`. The diff would be smaller, but `error.cause` becomes a `StandardSchemaV1Error` instead of a `ZodError`, which breaks the documented formatter. It may suit a major version better.
2. Put the proposal above behind an option on `initTRPC.create()`, if you want the default behaviour left untouched.
3. In userland, `.input((v) => schema.parse(v))` takes the `ParserCustomValidatorEsque` branch. It works, but it has to be repeated on every procedure, and a function parser takes its input type from its return type, so a schema with a transform loses its input type. Adapters that read the schema statically cannot use it at all: nestjs-trpc requires a bare schema in its decorators. I proposed the change there first (KevinEdry/nestjs-trpc#120, KevinEdry/nestjs-trpc#121). The maintainer would rather not change the parse path in an adapter and pointed at `getParseFn`.

### Additional information

<details>
<summary>Benchmark script</summary>

Run once as is and once with `compile` as argument. Each payload holds distinct objects: reusing one object reference across the array skews the compiled numbers.

```js
const compile = process.argv[2] === 'compile';
if (compile) await import('zod/compile');
const { z } = await import('zod');

const Item = z.object({
  id: z.string().uuid(), name: z.string().min(1).max(120), email: z.string().email(),
  role: z.enum(['admin', 'member', 'viewer']), score: z.number().min(0).max(100),
  active: z.boolean(), tags: z.array(z.string()).optional(),
  createdAt: z.union([z.date(), z.string()]), updatedAt: z.union([z.date(), z.string()]),
});
const Page = z.object({ items: z.array(Item), total: z.number().int(), hasMore: z.boolean() });
const item = {
  id: '3f2504e0-4f89-11d3-9a0c-0305e82c3301', name: 'Ada', email: 'ada@example.com',
  role: 'admin', score: 42, active: true, tags: ['a', 'b'],
  createdAt: '2026-01-01T00:00:00Z', updatedAt: '2026-01-02T00:00:00Z',
};

const fns = {
  parseAsync: Page.parseAsync.bind(Page), // what getParseFn binds today
  standard: async (v) => {                // getParseFn's Standard Schema branch
    const r = await Page['~standard'].validate(v);
    if (r.issues) throw new Error('issues');
    return r.value;
  },
  parse: Page.parse.bind(Page),
};

const med = (a) => [...a].sort((x, y) => x - y)[Math.floor(a.length / 2)];
for (const n of [1, 10, 100, 1000]) {
  const pool = Array.from({ length: 4 }, () => ({
    items: Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => ({ ...item, name: `Ada ${i}`, tags: [...item.tags] })),
    total: n,
    hasMore: false,
  }));
  const iters = Math.max(30, Math.round(20000 / n));
  const r = Object.fromEntries(Object.keys(fns).map((k) => [k, []]));
  for (let k = 0; k < 7; k++) {
    for (const [name, f] of Object.entries(fns)) {
      for (let i = 0; i < iters; i++) await f(pool[i % 4]);
      const t = process.hrtime.bigint();
      for (let i = 0; i < iters; i++) await f(pool[i % 4]);
      r[name].push(Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - t) / iters / 1000);
    }
  }
  console.log(n, Object.entries(r).map(([k, v]) => `${k} ${med(v).toFixed(2)} µs`).join('  '));
}
```

</details>

### 👨‍👧‍👦 Contributing

- [x] 🙋‍♂️ Yes, I'd be down to file a PR implementing this feature!

